{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "VIIRS_active_fire_loader",
    "description": "Pure-I/O data loader for NASA FIRMS VIIRS active-fire detections. Provides per-overpass point detections (one row per fire pixel per satellite pass): fire radiative power, brightness temperature, and detection confidence at irregular (lon,lat) detection locations resolved at runtime. This is the SUB-DAILY fire-front timing reference for fire-model validation (campfire-e2e Phase E1/E2) — the best progression signal between daily perimeters.\n\nThis is a `kind: points` loader: detections are NOT a fixed lattice — they are points discovered at runtime from the FIRMS area API (bbox + day filtered). Like the openaq_loader, this points source carries NO grid block — at 0.7.0 because 'any loader whose native grid is resolved at runtime may omit it', and since 0.8.0 because no data source may carry one at all; the detections' geographic (longlat/WGS84) coordinate reference is recorded in this file's metadata for provenance and to drive the owning model's identity reprojection by convention.\n\nThis is a pure-I/O data-source CATALOG: it declares its source (url_template) and its temporal cadence. It declares NO variables: esm 1.0.0 moved every former loader variable onto the model parameter that consumes it, and this catalog has no consumer, so its three file_variable declarations are parked verbatim in metadata.unconsumed_file_variables (see metadata.unconsumed_file_variables_note; a shared catalog is not self-describing at 1.0.0). It performs NO point-to-grid aggregation and NO regridding — those are the per-variable concern of an owning regridding model, if one is added (the openaq.esm cell-average pattern). Observed-fire loaders are VALIDATION refs — they do NOT feed the model RHS, so this loader has no companion forcing wrapper; the E2 harness compares detection timing/location to the simulated fire front. Because a `temporal` block is present, this loader seeds DISCRETE cadence (ess-v9a.5): the runtime refreshes the per-overpass detections on schedule.\n\nUSER-ACTION (campfire-e2e plan §User-action): a free FIRMS MAP_KEY is required for the area API. The url_template carries a {map_key} substitution; set it at runtime (do NOT commit a key). See metadata.access_note. Per the plan, if the points-loader kind proves immature for this use, the fallback is to rasterize detections to a detection_count(lon,lat,time) grid (the same offline trick as the NIFC loader); the framework-gaps report records the feasibility verdict.",
    "tags": [
      "earthsci_data",
      "wildfire",
      "active_fire",
      "fire_detections",
      "progression",
      "observations",
      "validation",
      "data_loader",
      "pure-io",
      "viirs",
      "modis",
      "firms",
      "point_observations",
      "longlat"
    ],
    "created": "2026-06-26T00:00:00Z",
    "authors": [
      "NASA FIRMS (Fire Information for Resource Management System)",
      "EarthSciModels campfire-e2e (esm-1zj)"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Schroeder, W., Oliva, P., Giglio, L., & Csiszar, I. A. (2014). The New VIIRS 375 m active fire detection data product: Algorithm description and initial assessment. Remote Sensing of Environment, 143, 85-96.",
        "url": "https://firms.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov/",
        "doi": "10.1016/j.rse.2013.12.008"
      },
      {
        "citation": "NASA FIRMS Fire Information for Resource Management System — Active Fire / Hotspot data (VIIRS S-NPP/NOAA-20 375 m + MODIS 1 km). Area API: https://firms.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov/api/area/. Requires a free MAP_KEY."
      }
    ]
  },
  "data_sources": {
    "VIIRS_active_fire": {
      "kind": "points",
      "source": {
        "url_template": "https://firms.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov/api/area/csv/{map_key}/{firms_source}/{bbox_west},{bbox_south},{bbox_east},{bbox_north}/{day_range}/{date:%Y-%m-%d}"
      },
      "temporal": {
        "start": "2012-01-20T00:00:00Z",
        "end": "2099-12-31T23:59:59Z",
        "file_period": "P1D",
        "frequency": "PT12H",
        "records_per_file": "auto",
        "time_variable": "acq_date"
      },
      "reference": {
        "citation": "Schroeder, W. et al. (2014). The New VIIRS 375 m active fire detection data product. Remote Sensing of Environment, 143, 85-96. NASA FIRMS: https://firms.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov/",
        "url": "https://firms.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov/",
        "doi": "10.1016/j.rse.2013.12.008"
      },
      "metadata": {
        "tags": [
          "viirs",
          "modis",
          "firms",
          "active_fire",
          "fire_detections",
          "point_observations",
          "progression",
          "validation"
        ],
        "native_crs": {
          "projection": "longlat",
          "datum": "WGS84",
          "proj4": "+proj=longlat +datum=WGS84 +no_defs",
          "note": "Geographic (unprojected) coordinate reference of the detection points. Recorded here for provenance because this points loader omits the formal GDD `grid` block (the native point set is resolved at runtime via the FIRMS area API). An owning regridding model would select the longlat IDENTITY reprojection by convention and bin detections into target cells (the openaq.esm cell-average pattern)."
        },
        "coordinate_columns": "The detection longitude/latitude are FIRMS CSV columns 'longitude'/'latitude', and the timestamp is 'acq_date' (YYYY-MM-DD) + 'acq_time' (HHMM UTC). These arrive in the points result's raw `records` (all columns are preserved); only the declared measurement variables (frp, brightness, confidence) are surfaced in `result.variables`. The consumer reads lon/lat/acq_date/acq_time from `records` to place each detection in space-time. acq_time gives the sub-daily front-timing resolution.",
        "firms_source_values": "{firms_source} selects the product: 'VIIRS_SNPP_NRT'/'VIIRS_SNPP_SP' (S-NPP 375 m), 'VIIRS_NOAA20_NRT'/'VIIRS_NOAA21_NRT', or 'MODIS_NRT'/'MODIS_SP' (1 km). NRT = near-real-time (last ~2 months); SP = standard-processing archive (older). For the 2018 Camp Fire (2018-11) use a VIIRS *_SP archive product.",
        "url_substitutions": "{map_key} = free FIRMS MAP_KEY (runtime-only, NOT committed); {firms_source} = product id above; {bbox_west,south,east,north} = domain bounding box in decimal degrees (WGS84, order W,S,E,N); {day_range} = number of days per request (1..10, here 1 for daily files); {date:%Y-%m-%d} = the day. Custom substitutions are passed through by the toolkit url_template layer.",
        "csv_columns": "latitude,longitude,bright_ti4,scan,track,acq_date,acq_time,satellite,instrument,confidence,version,bright_ti5,frp,daynight (VIIRS area-CSV product).",
        "frequency_note": "VIIRS S-NPP/NOAA-20 give ~2 overpasses/day per satellite (≈day + night), so frequency PT12H is the nominal per-satellite overpass cadence; the actual detection count per day is data-dependent (records_per_file 'auto'). file_period P1D = one area-API request per day.",
        "cadence_note": "temporal present → DISCRETE cadence (ess-v9a.5). temporal.start 2012-01-20 = FIRMS VIIRS S-NPP archive start (MODIS extends back to 2000-11 via MODIS products).",
        "fallback_note": "campfire-e2e plan: if the kind:points loader proves immature for fire-front validation, rasterize detections to detection_count(lon,lat,time) on the analysis grid (same offline trick as nifc_perimeters_loader) and read as kind:grid. The framework-gaps report records the F-data-4 feasibility verdict for the points path.",
        "use": "Sub-daily fire-front timing — finer than the daily NIFC perimeters; complements MTBS (final extent) and NIFC (daily progression).",
        "access_note": "NASA FIRMS active-fire data is free/open (cite Schroeder et al. 2014 and the FIRMS program); the area API REQUIRES a free MAP_KEY from https://firms.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov/api/area/. USER-ACTION: obtain a MAP_KEY and supply it at runtime via the {map_key} substitution (e.g. an env var) — do NOT commit a key. Like the era5/openaq loaders, the toolkit does not yet resolve a local data cache (EARTHSCIDATADIR) at the URL layer (see the observed-fire framework-gaps report); cache responses or use a file:// path until that C2 mechanism lands.",
        "user_action": "Obtain a free FIRMS MAP_KEY (campfire-e2e plan §User-action) and supply it at runtime; do not commit it.",
        "unconsumed_file_variables": {
          "frp": {
            "file_variable": "frp",
            "units": "MW",
            "description": "Fire Radiative Power (megawatts) of the detected fire pixel — proxy for combustion/energy-release rate. FIRMS VIIRS CSV column 'frp', already in MW (no unit conversion applied: the points loader's column mapping does not coerce string CSV cells, so numeric scaling is deferred to the consumer/parser — see the framework-gaps report)."
          },
          "brightness": {
            "file_variable": "bright_ti4",
            "units": "K",
            "description": "Brightness temperature (kelvin) of the fire pixel in VIIRS I-4 channel (~3.74 µm). FIRMS VIIRS CSV column 'bright_ti4', already in K. (MODIS equivalent is column 'brightness'; set file_variable accordingly when firms_source is a MODIS product.) The companion 'bright_ti5' (I-5, ~11.45 µm) is available in the raw records for the day/night and saturation tests."
          },
          "confidence": {
            "file_variable": "confidence",
            "units": "1",
            "description": "Detection confidence (dimensionless class). For VIIRS this is a CATEGORICAL flag in {'l' low, 'n' nominal, 'h' high} (FIRMS CSV column 'confidence'); for MODIS it is an integer 0-100. units '1' marks it dimensionless; the value is a category/string, NOT a measured quantity — consumers should map l/n/h → ordinal or filter on it. No unit_conversion (categorical)."
          }
        },
        "unconsumed_file_variables_note": "esm 1.0.0 removed the loader `variables` map: a data source is no longer a component, and every former loader variable becomes a PARAMETER on the model that CONSUMES it, carrying `update: {kind: \"data\", source: \"<registry key>\", from: {file_variable, unit_conversion?, codes?, select?}}` with `units` and `shape` declared once on that parameter (esm-spec §8). This file is a SHARED CATALOG with NO consuming .esm — nothing in the corpus mounts it; it is read only by tools/leaves/nifc_perimeters_rasterize.py and test/observed_fire_loaders_feasibility.py — so there is no consuming parameter to carry these declarations, and the 1.0.0 DataSource (additionalProperties:false over {kind, source, temporal, determinism, reader_options, select, record_filter, extent, reference, metadata}) has no `variables` key. The map is therefore parked VERBATIM in `unconsumed_file_variables` above so that not one file_variable, unit or description is lost. This is a demotion from a structured field to metadata, NOT an equivalent: a 1.0.0 consumer still has to re-declare each entry as a parameter `update.from.file_variable` + `units` before the runtime can read this source (earthsci_ast.load_data takes `bindings` = {consuming parameter name -> DataSourceBinding}, which a catalog with no consumer cannot supply). Recorded as the 'a shared catalog is not self-describing' reversion.",
        "migration_note": "esm 0.x -> 1.0.0: renamed the top-level `data_loaders` key to `data_sources` and removed this source's `variables` map — see unconsumed_file_variables_note. A data_sources-only catalog (no `models`) is a valid 1.0.0 document and loads through earthsci_ast.load; it is simply not mountable as a subsystem, which is correct — a data source is not a component, not a coupling endpoint and not a scoped-name path root."
      }
    }
  }
}
